1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,360
Hey friends and welcome to the Riker Medical Podcast.

2
00:00:04,360 --> 00:00:09,640
Riker Medical is a platform for education focused on emergency, transport, and critical

3
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:15,280
care medicine.

4
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:20,360
We believe that the sharing of knowledge should be free, accessible to all, and that innovative

5
00:00:20,360 --> 00:00:23,860
use of technology can facilitate that effort.

6
00:00:23,860 --> 00:00:29,060
This podcast is just one piece of that whole project.

7
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:36,960
Also, while we may mention medical treatments and interventions along the way, remember

8
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:40,700
that we are just strangers on the internet and don't do things you aren't allowed to

9
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:45,860
do.

10
00:00:45,860 --> 00:01:12,500
For more information about the project, visit us at RikerMedical.com.

11
00:01:15,860 --> 00:01:20,900
Thanks for joining.

12
00:01:20,900 --> 00:01:21,900
Thanks for having me.

13
00:01:21,900 --> 00:01:22,900
Yeah, for sure.

14
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:28,940
So yeah, I guess I just thought we could have a chat.

15
00:01:28,940 --> 00:01:32,980
I think I have some questions for you and then you might have some questions for me.

16
00:01:32,980 --> 00:01:38,260
We don't really have a lot of structure to this, but cool.

17
00:01:38,260 --> 00:01:44,980
So I guess the first thing I was curious about is you are relatively new to flying and how

18
00:01:44,980 --> 00:01:48,740
did you get here?

19
00:01:48,740 --> 00:02:03,060
Well let's see, I started in the ICU in 2016 and there was a few years of drowning in the

20
00:02:03,060 --> 00:02:10,020
education and all the things that we do in the ICU.

21
00:02:10,020 --> 00:02:18,380
As I got more, I think a theme I was thinking of before I came over, it was the concept

22
00:02:18,380 --> 00:02:22,820
of fake it till you make it.

23
00:02:22,820 --> 00:02:28,700
That point it seemed like I was pretty confident in what I was doing and even though internally

24
00:02:28,700 --> 00:02:34,180
like I didn't feel that way, I think one of my co-workers at the time was like, yeah,

25
00:02:34,180 --> 00:02:38,980
I have been flying for a couple of years, you should start flying.

26
00:02:38,980 --> 00:02:42,740
And then it was like, oh, you think I'm ready for that?

27
00:02:42,740 --> 00:02:46,940
And then she was like, yeah, you should apply, here's the application.

28
00:02:46,940 --> 00:02:56,220
So I started flying for a fix me company and it was all PRN at the time, which was kind

29
00:02:56,220 --> 00:03:01,300
of cool for me because I was working full time in the ICU and then I would have my extra

30
00:03:01,300 --> 00:03:03,740
days of being on call.

31
00:03:04,740 --> 00:03:11,140
Whether we did something or not, you get paid while you're home, so that was pretty sweet.

32
00:03:11,140 --> 00:03:13,340
I always knew that I wanted to go into flight.

33
00:03:13,340 --> 00:03:19,340
When I got into nursing school, it was like, okay, eventually I'm going to do flight.

34
00:03:19,340 --> 00:03:28,620
So I worked there for like four years or so before I came to Air Mythids.

35
00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:35,500
The last year, it's completely different from what I was doing before in a good way,

36
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:36,500
I think.

37
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:43,500
But yeah, to answer your question more shortly, I always knew I wanted to fly.

38
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:45,540
That's kind of always been a goal of mine.

39
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:51,180
And the hospital you're working at, you were in an ICU and as a trauma surgical ICU, right

40
00:03:51,180 --> 00:03:52,180
on.

41
00:03:52,180 --> 00:03:55,180
Yeah, so yeah, you know what that's like.

42
00:03:55,180 --> 00:03:56,180
For sure.

43
00:03:56,180 --> 00:03:58,180
And we would do everything.

44
00:03:58,740 --> 00:04:02,580
Yeah, and then some background for everyone, like Morgan and I work together in the ICU

45
00:04:02,580 --> 00:04:06,380
and now we work at the same base for Care Flight here in Santa Fe.

46
00:04:06,380 --> 00:04:11,300
Or up there in Santa Fe, elsewhere in Albuquerque.

47
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:17,340
So how has it been like the transition from like bedside nursing into like fixed-wing

48
00:04:17,340 --> 00:04:18,340
flight and then into rotors?

49
00:04:18,340 --> 00:04:22,740
Because I think that's a question that like a lot of people have when they're considering

50
00:04:22,740 --> 00:04:27,020
whether they want to get into this whole scene in general.

51
00:04:27,020 --> 00:04:38,180
Yeah, I think it was pretty similar to starting out in a sub-acute floor and going to an ICU,

52
00:04:38,180 --> 00:04:48,300
like a busy ICU where you have these expectations and there's just things that you're expected

53
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:50,940
to know and do in the ICU.

54
00:04:50,940 --> 00:04:55,420
Kind of similar to me in that sense going from fixed-wing to rotor.

55
00:04:55,420 --> 00:05:02,180
It was like, okay, now you've learned some things here, apply that and then add on top

56
00:05:02,180 --> 00:05:11,340
of all of the other aeromedical stuff, aeronautical stuff, flight physiology and logistics that

57
00:05:11,340 --> 00:05:18,180
you just don't see in an ICU or in a hospital or a space.

58
00:05:18,180 --> 00:05:22,500
The pre-hospital stuff is, it was quite overwhelming for me at first.

59
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:23,500
Yeah.

60
00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:25,740
It's also like the different patient populations.

61
00:05:25,740 --> 00:05:33,260
In the nursing side, we tend to be pigeonholed into one specific patient set.

62
00:05:33,260 --> 00:05:34,980
In our ICU, we never see kids.

63
00:05:34,980 --> 00:05:35,980
We rarely see kids.

64
00:05:35,980 --> 00:05:36,980
Right.

65
00:05:36,980 --> 00:05:37,980
Yeah.

66
00:05:37,980 --> 00:05:39,020
Definitely not babies.

67
00:05:39,020 --> 00:05:40,020
Or pregnant women.

68
00:05:40,020 --> 00:05:41,020
Yeah, totally.

69
00:05:41,020 --> 00:05:49,140
I remember like a few times where you get a younger kid that comes into the ICU or pregnant

70
00:05:49,140 --> 00:05:57,180
woman or post hemorrhage or whatever.

71
00:05:57,180 --> 00:05:59,340
It's like, well, what do you mean?

72
00:05:59,340 --> 00:06:02,420
I don't know what I'm doing here.

73
00:06:02,420 --> 00:06:04,420
Yeah, for sure.

74
00:06:04,420 --> 00:06:06,420
Yeah.

75
00:06:06,420 --> 00:06:16,020
I think that aspect has been challenging for sure in a rotor setting.

76
00:06:16,020 --> 00:06:20,300
You're still getting fed information with a fire hose.

77
00:06:20,300 --> 00:06:29,460
It's only been a little over a year for me in that arena, so it still feels pretty.

78
00:06:29,460 --> 00:06:32,420
It can feel overwhelming sometimes.

79
00:06:32,420 --> 00:06:36,700
Currently I just got through a bunch of quarterly innovations and quarterly stuff that we have

80
00:06:36,700 --> 00:06:37,700
to do.

81
00:06:37,700 --> 00:06:45,500
It's like stressing to get that done before the 20th while studying for my CFRN.

82
00:06:46,500 --> 00:06:47,500
When you test?

83
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:48,500
Next month.

84
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:49,500
Nice.

85
00:06:49,500 --> 00:06:50,500
Yeah.

86
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:55,260
I'm not looking forward to it.

87
00:06:55,260 --> 00:06:58,820
Yes, it's a necessary evil in the process.

88
00:06:58,820 --> 00:07:05,620
It's weird to do a certification that your job requires you to have.

89
00:07:05,620 --> 00:07:12,220
There's this added pressure of like, yeah, if I fail again, then I lose my job?

90
00:07:12,220 --> 00:07:13,220
That sucks.

91
00:07:13,220 --> 00:07:16,660
Yeah, because I guess normally you would get the certification first, so it's behind

92
00:07:16,660 --> 00:07:19,540
you before you transition into the job.

93
00:07:19,540 --> 00:07:21,860
Interesting.

94
00:07:21,860 --> 00:07:24,300
Did you have your CFRN before you started flying?

95
00:07:24,300 --> 00:07:25,300
I did.

96
00:07:25,300 --> 00:07:31,940
Well, I kind of went about it in a different way because I flew as a medic first.

97
00:07:31,940 --> 00:07:38,340
I had my FPC already before I got my nursing degree.

98
00:07:38,340 --> 00:07:45,980
At the time we had a different transition process for medics to become nurses.

99
00:07:45,980 --> 00:07:51,260
I was a nurse and was working in both the ER and ICU, but I was still getting paid in

100
00:07:51,260 --> 00:07:53,820
flight as a medic.

101
00:07:53,820 --> 00:07:58,020
In protest, I just took all my advanced certs and was like, you guys should pay me more.

102
00:07:58,020 --> 00:07:59,020
They were like, no.

103
00:07:59,020 --> 00:08:02,580
I was like, cool, I'm going to leave.

104
00:08:02,580 --> 00:08:04,580
Then let me come back eventually.

105
00:08:04,580 --> 00:08:06,140
That's why I did it that way.

106
00:08:06,140 --> 00:08:13,620
I thought maybe I could shift the seas of the giant company if I like, straw-garb them

107
00:08:13,620 --> 00:08:15,220
in with all my pieces of paper.

108
00:08:15,220 --> 00:08:18,660
They're very sad in their ways.

109
00:08:18,660 --> 00:08:19,660
Yeah, totally.

110
00:08:19,660 --> 00:08:20,660
That's okay.

111
00:08:20,660 --> 00:08:23,500
They let me come back eventually, so it's fine.

112
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:31,260
I think in that, just hearing your story about it, it seems compared to where I'm at right

113
00:08:31,260 --> 00:08:37,020
now mentally, I'm freaking out about this certification.

114
00:08:37,020 --> 00:08:39,340
I failed once already.

115
00:08:39,340 --> 00:08:45,180
I'm re-studying, doing it again, and going to retest.

116
00:08:45,180 --> 00:08:47,220
You had your FPC.

117
00:08:47,220 --> 00:08:50,660
You just went to nurses who were like, well, I'm just going to take this test now.

118
00:08:50,660 --> 00:08:53,460
Yeah, it's safe stuff.

119
00:08:53,460 --> 00:08:58,500
I think I'm also pretty lucky in that that I've always done pretty well at taking tests.

120
00:08:58,500 --> 00:09:02,060
I recognize that is not the case for a lot of people.

121
00:09:02,060 --> 00:09:03,060
Sure.

122
00:09:03,060 --> 00:09:06,100
I think BPA had an unfair advantage in that.

123
00:09:06,100 --> 00:09:10,060
Yeah, I mean, it is what it is, right?

124
00:09:10,060 --> 00:09:11,060
Yeah, totally.

125
00:09:11,060 --> 00:09:12,060
Everybody has their own thing.

126
00:09:12,060 --> 00:09:13,900
I've always struggled taking tests.

127
00:09:13,900 --> 00:09:15,900
It's just who I am.

128
00:09:15,900 --> 00:09:17,260
You'll figure it out.

129
00:09:17,260 --> 00:09:18,260
I'm not worried.

130
00:09:18,260 --> 00:09:20,260
Yeah, it'll be fine.

131
00:09:20,260 --> 00:09:21,260
Really.

132
00:09:21,260 --> 00:09:28,420
Then another question I had for you is, you did all of your nursing time in the...

133
00:09:28,660 --> 00:09:29,660
inpatient side.

134
00:09:29,660 --> 00:09:36,780
How do you think about, like in hindsight, do you think there's value in getting ER experience

135
00:09:36,780 --> 00:09:37,780
as well?

136
00:09:37,780 --> 00:09:42,260
Most people come to flight either with ER experience or ICU experience.

137
00:09:42,260 --> 00:09:46,940
Occasionally, we see people have both, but normally, most nurses follow one track or

138
00:09:46,940 --> 00:09:47,940
the other.

139
00:09:47,940 --> 00:09:51,940
What are your thoughts on that?

140
00:09:51,940 --> 00:09:56,900
I think it would depend on where you're at.

141
00:09:57,900 --> 00:10:05,420
I've talked to a few people recently that work full time in the ER and don't really

142
00:10:05,420 --> 00:10:16,540
have a lot of experience with titrating drips or using multiple drips to get a certain effect

143
00:10:16,540 --> 00:10:19,740
or a certain outcome.

144
00:10:19,740 --> 00:10:28,500
I think it would be pretty helpful to have more experience with pediatrics because that's

145
00:10:28,500 --> 00:10:33,420
always a source of contention with people in general.

146
00:10:33,420 --> 00:10:38,340
I think the universal thought is, peeds are just challenging in general.

147
00:10:38,340 --> 00:10:39,340
Yeah, kids are scary.

148
00:10:39,340 --> 00:10:40,340
Yeah.

149
00:10:40,340 --> 00:10:41,340
Sure.

150
00:10:41,340 --> 00:10:48,340
I personally, had I thought about that ahead of time, I probably would have tried to do

151
00:10:49,340 --> 00:10:56,340
some more time in pediatrics, not so much in like NICU specific or working in the ER

152
00:10:58,420 --> 00:11:04,860
in the hopes that I would get some peeds here and there, but actually, go work in the PICU.

153
00:11:04,860 --> 00:11:07,820
Go work in the Peds ED.

154
00:11:07,820 --> 00:11:10,820
That I think would be beneficial.

155
00:11:10,820 --> 00:11:16,820
Yeah, especially at a hospital like UNM where they have a dedicated PDR, so it's an available

156
00:11:16,820 --> 00:11:17,820
option.

157
00:11:18,820 --> 00:11:22,820
The flip side of that though, I think is, does it really matter?

158
00:11:22,820 --> 00:11:23,820
No.

159
00:11:23,820 --> 00:11:24,820
For sure.

160
00:11:24,820 --> 00:11:25,820
Yeah, that's one thing.

161
00:11:25,820 --> 00:11:29,820
I don't know if we ever had this conversation when you were looking to come fly.

162
00:11:29,820 --> 00:11:36,220
All you have to do is I'm oversimplifying here, but to get into a flight program is

163
00:11:36,220 --> 00:11:40,220
you have to demonstrate that you're not an asshole and that you're trainable.

164
00:11:40,220 --> 00:11:45,220
Then you get along with people and they will teach you the things that you don't know yet.

165
00:11:45,220 --> 00:11:49,420
But expecting people to know how to operate in an aircraft when they've never done it

166
00:11:49,420 --> 00:11:50,420
is unfair.

167
00:11:50,420 --> 00:11:55,420
Expecting someone to know how to take care of a sick OB patient or a kid when they've

168
00:11:55,420 --> 00:11:58,420
never done it, that's unrealistic.

169
00:11:58,420 --> 00:12:05,420
Also, even when you train, I think we're going to get to this, but I got lucky I think in

170
00:12:07,660 --> 00:12:14,660
that I had a lot of time to train in a rotor during my orientation.

171
00:12:14,780 --> 00:12:16,100
People don't get that at all.

172
00:12:16,100 --> 00:12:20,820
They're training on fixed wing and then they're coming to a rotor base and they're like,

173
00:12:20,820 --> 00:12:23,340
well, I've never even flown in a helicopter.

174
00:12:23,340 --> 00:12:25,380
How do you expect me to work now?

175
00:12:25,380 --> 00:12:30,780
Yeah, that's how it was for me because when I started here, we didn't have any, we only

176
00:12:30,780 --> 00:12:33,140
had A-stars in the state.

177
00:12:33,140 --> 00:12:36,580
All of my orientation was a fixed wing.

178
00:12:36,580 --> 00:12:40,980
Your first rotor shift is the first time you're putting on your helmet, NVGs and going to

179
00:12:40,980 --> 00:12:42,060
a scene flight.

180
00:12:42,060 --> 00:12:47,540
I was lucky that I had some background in the EMS side, but I can't imagine coming into

181
00:12:47,540 --> 00:12:54,540
that scene, not having done that, trying to manage your first scene without ...

182
00:12:55,780 --> 00:13:02,780
I think that's what I came to realize is the more challenging part of Air Medical is the

183
00:13:02,780 --> 00:13:07,780
stuff that you just have no exposure to.

184
00:13:07,780 --> 00:13:12,780
When I went on my first scene call, luckily I was with somebody that knew what they were

185
00:13:12,780 --> 00:13:18,780
doing because I was just like this lost puppy wandering around like, yeah, I can carry that.

186
00:13:18,780 --> 00:13:19,780
I can do that.

187
00:13:19,780 --> 00:13:22,780
We're like, sure, sure, whatever.

188
00:13:22,780 --> 00:13:23,780
Totally.

189
00:13:23,780 --> 00:13:27,780
Just like running around, not really being efficient in any way.

190
00:13:27,780 --> 00:13:28,780
Yeah.

191
00:13:29,780 --> 00:13:34,780
I think it's also worth remembering that that's fine.

192
00:13:34,780 --> 00:13:35,780
Yeah.

193
00:13:35,780 --> 00:13:41,780
I think we tend to have this perception, we as ground EMS people and nurse in the hospital,

194
00:13:41,780 --> 00:13:43,780
that the flight crew is like ...

195
00:13:43,780 --> 00:13:44,780
I know exactly what they're doing.

196
00:13:44,780 --> 00:13:45,780
Yeah, they're on it all the time.

197
00:13:45,780 --> 00:13:46,780
Yeah.

198
00:13:46,780 --> 00:13:47,780
They just wear cool pajamas.

199
00:13:47,780 --> 00:13:48,780
Yeah.

200
00:13:48,780 --> 00:13:49,780
That's it.

201
00:13:49,780 --> 00:13:50,780
Yeah.

202
00:13:50,780 --> 00:13:54,780
It has been an interesting experience to see that firsthand too.

203
00:13:54,780 --> 00:14:01,780
I walk into a room and I'm like, shitting my pants about what we're about to do and

204
00:14:01,780 --> 00:14:02,780
what's going to happen.

205
00:14:02,780 --> 00:14:04,780
Everybody's looking at you like, okay, what's next?

206
00:14:04,780 --> 00:14:06,780
I'm like, I don't know.

207
00:14:06,780 --> 00:14:07,780
Yeah.

208
00:14:07,780 --> 00:14:13,780
Sometimes you just got to do something or convince everyone else to do nothing.

209
00:14:13,780 --> 00:14:15,780
Just like make a decision one way or the other.

210
00:14:15,780 --> 00:14:20,780
You don't necessarily have to do stuff, but voice a decision and it's probably going to

211
00:14:20,780 --> 00:14:21,780
be fine.

212
00:14:21,780 --> 00:14:27,780
Then going back to this fake it till you make it concept.

213
00:14:27,780 --> 00:14:39,780
I think it's very useful and it can be pretty prevalent in critical care space in general.

214
00:14:39,780 --> 00:14:43,780
It totally helped me get from where I was to where I am.

215
00:14:43,780 --> 00:14:50,780
In the process, I still struggle with imposter syndrome.

216
00:14:50,780 --> 00:14:56,780
There's a lot that I know and there's a whole lot that I don't know.

217
00:14:56,780 --> 00:14:57,780
Yeah.

218
00:14:57,780 --> 00:15:06,260
Then being able to recognize that and have the humility to be like, well, let's confer

219
00:15:06,260 --> 00:15:07,500
with someone else.

220
00:15:07,500 --> 00:15:12,460
Let's get some help from someone else or figure that out.

221
00:15:12,460 --> 00:15:17,180
I think is still a pretty key attribute to have.

222
00:15:17,180 --> 00:15:18,180
Yeah.

223
00:15:18,180 --> 00:15:20,180
I think that's normal.

224
00:15:20,180 --> 00:15:28,180
I think if you ever get to a situation where you never have any doubt about what you're

225
00:15:28,180 --> 00:15:35,020
doing, probably just not looking hard enough for sure.

226
00:15:35,020 --> 00:15:40,340
I think that is a healthy response because you don't want to be overconfident in what

227
00:15:40,340 --> 00:15:42,220
you're doing.

228
00:15:42,220 --> 00:15:44,940
Even for me, I've been flying for a long time now.

229
00:15:44,940 --> 00:15:53,260
I started flying in 2013 and I forgot the blood at the base the other day going on a

230
00:15:53,260 --> 00:15:54,260
seed flight.

231
00:15:54,260 --> 00:15:55,940
It's like, yeah, it happens.

232
00:15:55,940 --> 00:15:58,860
You make mistakes and it's part of the game.

233
00:15:58,860 --> 00:15:59,860
Yeah.

234
00:15:59,860 --> 00:16:00,860
But then owning it.

235
00:16:00,860 --> 00:16:01,860
Totally.

236
00:16:01,860 --> 00:16:03,020
The owning it is the.

237
00:16:03,020 --> 00:16:04,020
For sure.

238
00:16:04,020 --> 00:16:05,020
Yeah.

239
00:16:05,020 --> 00:16:07,020
We had to like, yeah, we figured it out.

240
00:16:07,020 --> 00:16:08,020
It was fine.

241
00:16:08,020 --> 00:16:09,020
Yeah.

242
00:16:09,020 --> 00:16:10,020
Pinching.

243
00:16:10,020 --> 00:16:11,020
Yeah.

244
00:16:11,020 --> 00:16:12,660
It's like, I've been out blood for a long time.

245
00:16:12,660 --> 00:16:13,660
Yeah.

246
00:16:13,660 --> 00:16:15,660
But does make you feel really silly and that's okay.

